_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 13
---
order: 13
---
short_label: II.13
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In acute-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the acute angle is less than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the acute angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the acute angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off within by the perpendicular towards the acute angle.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} be a triangle having the angle at {B} acute, and draw {AD} from the point {A} perpendicular to {BC}. [!just I.12]

I say that the square on {AC} is less than the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} by twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII13.gif" alt="II.13"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // II.13 is II.12's mirror, and this deck is built as its mirror: beat for
    // beat, the same colour and marker conventions, so a reader stepping
    // from one to the other sees the same argument with the perpendicular
    // falling INSIDE the base instead of outside it.
    //
    // As in II.12 no squares are drawn: on a 250x230 canvas the square on CB
    // (170px a side) or the rectangle CB by BD (170x70) would fall off it, so
    // refs stay LITERAL — {AC} lights the segment and "the square on" is
    // carried by the caption. Everything added is fully transparent (;0;0;0
    // lines, ;0;0;0;0 polygons), so the static figure is unchanged; the two
    // markers are ordinary coloured elements but showAngles defaults to false
    // (0.9.0+), so they arrive only with the walk.
    //
    // Colour: ABC keeps Joyce's random fill and is the CONTAINING figure —
    // D falls within BC, so ABC = ADB + ADC exactly, and the two parts light
    // one per slide (7 and 8) rather than repainting his fill. The explicit
    // ";0" faces are what stop a highlighted part painting a near-white
    // OPAQUE face over the triangle beneath it (#140). Markers follow I.47:
    // red = right angle, magenta = the angle the proposition is about.
    // Joyce's figure declares the perpendicular twice — AD (line;foot) and DA
    // (line;connect), the same black segment under two names, because the
    // proof writes it both ways. DA is therefore kept OUT of slide 2's set:
    // it would paint the segment from t=0 while AD, that slide's animation
    // target, is still held hidden. It joins from slide 3, where it is only
    // needed so that {DA} in a caption has something to light (hover emphasis
    // bails on visible = false), and where it adds no ink AD is not already
    // drawing.
    var TRI   = ["A","B","C","ABC","AB","BC","AC","angABC"];
    var PERP  = TRI.concat(["D","AD","BD","DC"]);
    var NAMED = PERP.concat(["DA"]);
    var RT1   = NAMED.concat(["angD","ADB"]);
    var RT2   = RT1.concat(["ADC"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "II.13",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;100,30",
            "B;point;free;30,200",
            "C;point;free;200,200",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "AD;line;foot;A,B,C",
            "D;point;last;AD",
            "DA;line;connect;D,A",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — the triangle above already
            // draws all three of these sides.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            // The two segments of the cut line CB — the II.7 step, and II.1's
            // whole-and-parts idiom.
            "BD;line;connect;B,D;0;0;0",
            "DC;line;connect;D,C;0;0;0",
            // The two right triangles the two I.47 steps are about. Together
            // they are the whole of ABC.
            "ADB;polygon;triangle;A,D,B;0;0;0;0",
            "ADC;polygon;triangle;A,D,C;0;0;0;0",
            // The acute angle of the hypothesis is angle ABC — but the
            // TRIANGLE owns the name "ABC", so the marker is angABC and there
            // is deliberately NO "ABC" alias (it would steal the triangle's
            // hover). A caption that needs to name the angle would use the
            // display override {ABC|angABC}; none does now — Joyce letters the
            // acute angle nowhere after slide 1, so the closing slides carry
            // the marker through their slide sets instead.
            //
            // Unlike II.12, one marker does NOT serve both right angles here:
            // D lies between B and C, so angle ADB and angle ADC are
            // supplementary, not equal. angD is drawn on the ADC side.
            "angABC;sector;angleMarker;B,A,C;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angD;sector;angleMarker;D,A,C;0;0;red;red"
        ],
        // No deferDraggables: A, B and C are the only draggables, and slide 1
        // introduces all three as the given triangle. D is the opposite case —
        // a derived point (point;last) is NOT auto-unioned, so it is listed
        // explicitly in every visible set from slide 2 on, or it loses its dot
        // and its label.
        aliases: { "CB": "BC", "BA": "AB", "CA": "AC",
                   "DB": "BD", "CD": "DC" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABC} be a triangle having the angle at {B} acute.",
              visible: TRI,
              // angABC is this slide's animation target, so it is deliberately
              // NOT highlighted (euclid#171). SlateAnimator hides an animated
              // target until its cascade step starts (#83) but sets
              // shouldHighlight at run start, and drawFace renders a hidden
              // element that is highlighted — so listing it here would paint
              // the wedge from t=0 and the sweep would have nothing to reveal.
              // The animation is the emphasis.
              highlighted: ["ABC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angABC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "less than a right angle", ref: "I.Def.12" }] },
            { text: "Draw {AD} from the point {A} perpendicular to {BC}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["BC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "perpendicular to BC", ref: "I.12" }] },
            { text: "I say that the square on {AC} is less than the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} by twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}.",
              visible: NAMED,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","AB","BD"] },
            { text: "Since the straight line {CB} has been cut at random at {D}, the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BD} equals twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD} plus the square on {DC}.",
              visible: NAMED,
              highlighted: ["BC","BD","DC","D"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the whole CB, cut at D", ref: "II.7" }] },
            // Joyce's two sentences here take a slide each rather than one
            // compressed caption (process.md, "split rather than compress").
            { text: "Add the square on {DA} to each.",
              visible: NAMED,
              highlighted: ["DA"] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {CB}, {BD}, and {DA} equals twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD} plus the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DC}.",
              visible: NAMED,
              highlighted: ["BC","BD","DC","AD"],
              // C.N.2 licenses the resulting equality, so it sits here rather
              // than on the instruction that precedes it.
              justifications: [{ claim: "the square on DA added to each", ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {BD} and {DA}, for the angle at {D} is right.",
              visible: RT1,
              highlighted: ["ADB","AB","BD","AD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right triangle ADB", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "And the square on {AC} equals the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DC}.",
              visible: RT2,
              // ADC is this slide's animation target — kept out of
              // highlighted, per the note on slide 1.
              highlighted: ["AC","AD","DC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "ADC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right triangle ADC, at the same right angle", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} equals the square on {AC} plus twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["BC","AB","AC","BD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "substitute and take away", ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "So that the square on {AC} alone is less than the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} by twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","AB","BD","angABC"] },
            // The closing enunciation, in Joyce's words. It is where the acute
            // angle is spoken of again, so the marker keeps the gold here. His
            // sentence letters no angle, so there is no {ABC|angABC} display
            // override to make: the slide set names angABC and that is all the
            // marker needs.
            { text: "Therefore in acute-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the acute angle is less than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the acute angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the acute angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off within by the perpendicular towards the acute angle.",
              visible: RT2,
              highlighted: ["AC","BC","AB","BD","angABC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Def.12": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI12/",
        "I.12":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI12/",
        "I.47":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "II.7":     "/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII7/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since the straight line {CB} has been cut at random at {D}, the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BD} equals twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD} plus the square on {DC}. [!just II.7]

Add the square on {DA} to each.  Therefore the sum of the squares on {CB}, {BD}, and {DA} equals twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD} plus the sum of the  squares on {AD} and {DC}.

But the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {BD} and  {DA}, for the angle at {D} is right,  and the square on {AC} equals the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DC}, therefore the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} equals the square on {AC} plus twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}, so that the square on {AC} alone is less than the sum of the squares on {CB} and {BA} by twice the rectangle {CB} by {BD}. [!just I.47]

Therefore *in acute-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the acute angle is less than the sum of the squares on the sides containing the acute angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the acute angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the straight line cut off within by the perpendicular towards the acute angle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
